{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Yu2014-Optik-stack18-nonpolarizing",
  "Source": "K. Yu, J. Yin, D. Huang, and J. Bao, \"Design of non-polarization angle-tuned narrowband thin film filter,\" Optik 125, 397-400 (2014). doi:10.1016/j.ijleo.2013.06.089 - Eq. (18), p. 399 (Sec. 3)",
  "Notes": "Final non-polarizing angle-tuned 100 GHz four-cavity filter: (HMLM)^m mirrors plus 5M2L5M spacers (effective spacer index N = 1.66) so s and p coincide in bandwidth and centre at 25 deg\nSpecification: Non-polarizing passband at 25 deg: s and p bandwidths and centre wavelengths coincide; tunable from 1567 nm (0 deg) to 1527 nm (25 deg).\nReported: Text p. 399 and Fig. 4: centre wavelength 1567 nm at 0 deg, shifting to 1527 nm at 25 deg; at 25 deg the average-light bandwidth at 25 dB is < 1.1 nm, at 0.5 dB > 0.35 nm, and the polarization dependent loss < 0.2 dB; s and p curves almost coincide (Fig. 4, axis 1525.5-1528.5 nm). Spacer effective index N = 1.66 for Eq. (16) lambda = lambda0 sqrt(1 - sin^2(theta)/N^2). Abstract/conclusion: tunable range > 40 nm, covers the C-band.\nNotes: \"5M2L5M\" = 5M, 2L, 5M. As printed the end M of (HMLM)^m and the first M of (MLMH)^m touch the 5M layers, so each spacer is physically 6M 2L 6M; the 255 layers as printed merge to 247 layers (291 quarter waves). No printed total. The stack is a palindrome, so which side faces the light does not change the layer order. Incident medium taken as air (n0 in Eqs. 4-15; the paper does not name it) and the 25 deg angle as in air. Substrate glass n_G = 1.523 (p. 397) given as an inline constant. H = Si with n_H = 3.86 as for stack (17) (same uncertainty: real Si ~3.48 at 1.55 um). Eq. (16) with N = 1.66 gives ~1515 nm at 25 deg, not the 1527 nm reported, so the reported shift comes from the full simulation.\nLayers from Eq. (18), p. 399 (Sec. 3); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours centre 1567.24 nm at 0 deg, 1528.36 nm at 25 deg (s 1528.57, p 1528.34); paper centre 1567 nm at 0 deg, 1527 nm at 25 deg; s and p almost coincide. Uses the paper’s Si n = 3.86 (real Si is about 3.48 at 1.55 um).",
  "Wavelength": [
    1567
  ],
  "Materials": [
